---
/**
 * /websites/ — the website build. THE THIRD PAID THING ON THIS SITE, and the
 * first page that argues against the thing most visitors are already using.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL, WHICH IS A REVERSAL. D55 (2026-08-30) decided the
 * website starting figure stays on client paper, and answered the anchor problem
 * with one sentence on /services/ and /main-street/: a site "carries its own
 * number, scoped and quoted before anything starts". That sentence is true and
 * it leaves a reader knowing only that a site costs more than the build floor. Somebody
 * comparing three quotes reads that as evasion, and the floor beside it — sized
 * for a fitted shelf tool — is the number they carry away instead.
 *
 * IT CLEARS THE THIRD-NUMBER TEST WRITTEN INTO src/lib/pricing.js, which allows
 * one only for a product a visitor could buy without buying either of the
 * others. A business can buy a site without buying a shelf tool or an
 * assessment, and most of them arrive wanting exactly that.
 *
 * THE PRICE AND ITS QUALIFIER ARE ONE OBJECT and the qualifier here is longer
 * than the other two on purpose. The entire argument for the website floor over
 * a monthly subscription is the six things it buys, so a page showing the
 * figure without them has published the objection and withheld the answer.
 *
 * THE COMPARISON NAMES COMPANIES, AND EVERY LEFT-HAND CLAIM IS REGISTERED.
 * `wix-no-site-export`, `squarespace-lapse-window` and `godaddy-ai-credits`
 * live in src/data/external-claims.js — they age, they warn, and either a person
 * or a browser run re-reads them. They cannot be probed: REACHABLE_HOSTS
 * defaults to no, and a green tick beside a competitor's terms would be the same
 * failure wearing a check mark.
 *
 * THE THIRD ONE USED TO BE `godaddy-no-app-market` AND IT WENT FALSE. GoDaddy
 * retired Websites + Marketing for an AI builder that advertises code export, so
 * a line on this page named a product nobody sells. It was the one claim of the
 * three sourced from a review site rather than the vendor, and its own note said
 * to confirm against GoDaddy's own pages before leaning harder on it. The
 * replacement was read off GoDaddy's plans table in a browser on 2026-09-10.
 *
 * THE FOUR ROWS ARE THE DOOR, NOT THE ARGUMENT. /websites/compare/ carries all
 * nine options a local business actually has, including the two that are free
 * and the one that hands over source code — none of which four rows can hold.
 *
 * ONE ARGUMENT IS REFUSED ON EVIDENCE AND MUST STAY REFUSED. Builder sites are
 * not slow — default-template mobile Core Web Vitals pass rates measured
 * 2026-09-03 run Wix 80.7%, Squarespace 70.2%, WordPress 49.3%. A speed claim
 * would be disprovable by any prospect with a free testing tool.
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import { websitePrice, startingPrice } from '../data/services';
import { websiteComparison } from '../data/home';
import { WEBSITE_USD } from '../lib/pricing.js';
/* THE SIX THINGS MOVED TO src/data/website-scope.js ON 2026-09-10 and this
   page now reads them rather than owning them. They still come straight off
   §1.1–§1.6 of docs/engagement/agreements/website-build-scope.md, and the
   scope is still the argument for the number. What changed is who else can
   see them: the printed comparison sheet is rendered by a Node script, which
   cannot read an .astro file at all, so the one document whose job is arguing
   what the figure buys had to answer it in a single borrowed sentence on its
   last page. */
import { included } from '../data/website-scope.js';
import { Check, X, ArrowUpRight } from '@lucide/astro';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;


const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'Service',
    '@id': `${origin}/websites/#service`,
    name: 'Website build',
    serviceType: 'Custom website design and development',
    provider: { '@id': `${origin}/#organization` },
    areaServed: 'US',
    description:
      'A website you own outright — your accounts, your domain, working email, a search listing, the two policy pages, and one working tool built into the site.',
    offers: {
      '@type': 'Offer',
      price: WEBSITE_USD,
      priceCurrency: 'USD',
      description: websitePrice.qualifier,
    },
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${origin}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Services', item: `${origin}/services/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Websites', item: `${origin}/websites/` },
    ],
  },
];
---
<MarketingLayout
  schema={schema}
  title="A website you own"
  description="A real website in your own accounts, not rented from a platform: working email, your search listing, the two policy pages the law requires, and one tool of your own built in."
  image="/og/websites.png"
>
  {/* ── Hero. The figure and its qualifier are one block, never a screen apart. */}
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-line">
    <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pt-16 pb-10 sm:pt-24 sm:pb-14">
      <p class="mono-label">A site in your name · yours outright · from {websitePrice.amount}</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-5xl">
        Renting a website is cheap. Here is what it <span class="aiq-accent">actually costs</span>.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        The monthly ones are genuinely cheap and they genuinely work. What is never on the pricing
        page is what happens the day you want to leave, and on most of them the answer is that you
        cannot — not with your pages, anyway.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        This is the other option. It costs more once and nothing after that, and at the end of it you
        are holding the thing rather than borrowing it.
      </p>
      {/* WHO IT IS FOR, NAMED. Moved here on 2026-09-11 from the home page's
          three-shapes section, which the operator cut. The sentence was the
          `instance` line on its website card — one real reader rather than a
          category — and this page argued renting against owning at length while
          never once saying whose problem that is. It had no home among the five
          build shapes on /services/, because having no website is not a size of
          build; it is the subject of this page. */}
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
        <span class="text-accent">→</span> For a business running on a Facebook page and a phone
        number.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Built in New Bern, North Carolina. A business in Craven County gets the kickoff in person;
        anywhere else gets the same build over video, on the same terms.
      </p>

      <div class="panel mt-8 max-w-2xl p-6">
        <p class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">{websitePrice.line}</p>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{websitePrice.qualifier}</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
          It is a floor because I quote the job rather than the category. I would rather be honest
          about where it starts than advertise a number I cannot hold.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <a href="/contact/?intent=quote" class="btn btn-primary">Ask what yours would cost →</a>
        <a href="/main-street/" class="btn btn-secondary">Three of these are free right now</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── What the number buys. This section IS the justification for the figure. */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
    <p class="mono-label">What the number buys</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-2xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
      Six things, and five of them are the ones people forget.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      Most quotes for a website cover the pages and stop there. Then the mail goes to junk, nobody
      can find you, and there is no policy page the day a form starts collecting names.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {included.map((s) => (
        <div class="panel lift flex flex-col gap-3 p-6">
          <span class="mono-label">{s.n}</span>
          <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{s.t}</h3>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{s.b}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── The comparison. Every left-hand line is a registered, dated claim. ─── */}
  <section class="section-band border-y border-line">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
      <p class="mono-label">The difference</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-2xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
        The question is not what it costs. It is what you are holding at the end.
      </h2>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="panel lift spotlight p-7">
          <p class="mono-label">{websiteComparison.left.title}</p>
          <ul class="mt-5 flex flex-col gap-3">
            {websiteComparison.left.items.map((item) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-ink-muted">
                <X size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-down" aria-hidden="true" />
                <span>{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="panel lift spotlight border-accent/40 p-7">
          <p class="mono-label text-cyan-deep">{websiteComparison.right.title}</p>
          <ul class="mt-5 flex flex-col gap-3">
            {websiteComparison.right.items.map((item) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-ink">
                <Check size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-up" aria-hidden="true" />
                <span>{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
        Every line on the left was read off those companies' own help and pricing pages, the first
        three on 3 September 2026 and the GoDaddy one on 10 September 2026 when the product it named
        was retired. Terms change, so if you are weighing this up, go and check them again rather
        than taking my word for it.
      </p>
      {/* THE DOOR INTO THE FULL FIELD. Four rows argue against a template
          subscription and are accurate about that; they miss the two free
          options and the one that hands over source code entirely. */}
      <div class="mt-7">
        <a href="/websites/compare/" class="btn btn-secondary">
          Every option side by side, including where they beat me
          <ArrowUpRight class="h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── What this is not. The /check/ and /ai-fit/ pattern. ────────────────── */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
    <p class="mono-label">What this is not</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
      Not a reason to leave something that is working.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      If you have a site you like, people find it when they search for you, and your mail arrives —
      then you do not need me, and I will say so on the call. The businesses this is for are running
      on a Facebook page, on nothing at all, or on a subscription they resent and cannot leave.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      It is also not a claim that those platforms are badly built, because they are not. What they
      will not do is hand you the thing, and they have nowhere to put the one piece of software your
      business actually runs on — which is the part you cannot buy monthly at any price.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      And it is not the only number here. A tool on its own is a smaller job and a smaller number:
      {' '}{startingPrice.line.toLowerCase()} {startingPrice.qualifier}
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 border-t border-line pt-10">
      <p class="mono-label">Not sure a site is the thing you need</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        Then buy the answer before you buy the build.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        The AI Fit Assessment is a read of how your work actually runs — on video, or in your
        business — and a written list of what is worth building with a price beside each line. If a
        website is not the first thing on that list, it will say so, and part of the fee comes off
        whatever you do build.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <a href="/contact/?intent=quote" class="btn btn-primary">
          Ask what yours would cost <ArrowUpRight class="h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
        <a href="/ai-fit/" class="btn btn-secondary">Read about the assessment</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>
